==== Wave 1: Strategy

===== The Story So Far

*The Cartographer.* You have learned to survive, and now the map is open. The thread of pale fire that woke you has grown into a web of routes: economy charts, freighter lanes, fleet schedules. You are no longer a survivor clinging to one world — you are the one who draws the lines between worlds, who knows which star pays for fuel and which planet hides the rare harvest. Wave 1 is where the universe stops being a backdrop and becomes a market, a fleet, a project.

This wave is a *new game*, harder than Wave 0: more systems live, the economy matters, and your choices compound.

===== The Cartographer's Economy

+ *Understand economies* — every star system has an economy type and a wealth level. Prices vary by system, and *trade routes* — buying in systems that produce cheap goods and selling where they are scarce — are the classic income engine. The space station's trade terminal and the galaxy map's economy filter are your tools.
+ *Buy low, sell high* — watch the market: goods sell for more in systems whose economy *consumes* them. A system map that lists economies (if you have the economy scanner) turns the whole region into a route.
+ *Craft for profit* — refining and crafting can turn cheap inputs into pricier products. The value-add loop is where the "no power spikes" philosophy lives on your side of the screen: the game's economy is the challenge, not the mods.
+ *Nanites as a second wallet* — nanites fund upgrades. Earn them by uploading scanned discoveries, completing missions, and interacting with the right NPCs. Never spend nanites on something you can buy with units.

#callout("Tip:", [
  The single most useful Wave 1 purchase is the *economy scanner* for your starship (from space stations). It turns the galaxy map from decoration into a price list.
])

===== Exploration & Discovery

+ *Scan everything, upload everything* — every creature, plant, and mineral you scan and upload pays. Completed planet surveys pay a bonus. This is steady, risk-free income that funds everything else.
+ *Chase the rare harvest* — planets with rare or exotic resources (activated metals, storm crystals, curious deposits) are where the real money is — and where the risk is: storms, sentinels, and harsh hazards.
+ *Mark what matters* — base locations, portal addresses, and good mining spots are your cartographer's notes. The game's waypoints and your own bases do this; keep your bookkeeping tidy.

===== Frigates & the Fleet

+ *The freighter is your mobile base* — claim a freighter when the game offers it (or buy one), and it becomes your command post: storage, fleet management, and a warp-capable home.
+ *Frigates run missions* — buy frigates, send them on expeditions from the fleet management console. They bring back units, resources, and rare finds. Frigate class and specialization matter; a balanced fleet covers more mission types.
+ *Fleet upkeep* — fuel the expeditions, repair damaged frigates, and level your frigates up through successful missions. A working fleet is a compounding asset — which is exactly why Wave 1 mods may deepen it but never inflate it.

===== Upgrade Priorities

+ *Exosuit first* — more inventory, more tech slots, movement upgrades (jetpack range and fuel). The suit is the platform every other upgrade stands on.
+ *Multi-tool* — one good multi-tool beats three average ones. Upgrade the mining beam and scanner first; combat is secondary at this stage.
+ *Starship* — class matters (C → B → A → S), and *S-class* is the prize. An A-class with good tech is worth more than a bare S-class; hunt upgrades, not just hulls.
+ *Tech modules* — purchased with nanites, installed in tech slots. Stacked modules of the same type boost each other; this is the real power curve, and it is the game's, not a mod's.

#callout("Tip:", [
  The Wave 1 power curve is *supposed* to come from the game: better ships, stacked modules, a growing fleet. If a mod proposal for this wave would skip any of that, it fails the wave's "no pure power spikes" rule — new systems yes, shortcuts no.
])

===== The Harder New Game

Wave 1 is a fresh save on a harder setting. The intended shape:

1. Start a new game — *Survival* (harsher hazards, scarcer resources) is the intended Wave 1 difficulty; *Permadeath* is the natural Wave 2 test.
2. Keep Wave 0's UI/QoL base enabled — it is difficulty-agnostic.
3. Enable Wave 1's content and mechanics mods only.
4. Play the economy and fleet loops deliberately: set an income target per session, not a grind.

#callout("Warning:", [
  A Wave 1 new game must not carry Wave 2's mods — the systems this wave teaches (economy, fleet) are the tutorial for Wave 2's depth. Check Singularity's enabled list before starting the save.
])
